ocks":[{"t":"p","x":"An educational, men-focused service led by Nurse James at Harmony Derma in Riyadh."},{"t":"ul","i":["A men's laser hair removal focus","Clear, educational treatment information","Respectful and discreet communication","Individual assessment and treatment planning","Attention to skin type and hair characteristics","Practical preparation and aftercare guidance","A convenient location in Al Hamra, Riyadh"]}]},{"id":"about","show":true,"title":"Meet Nurse James","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"Nurse James provides men's laser hair removal services at Harmony Derma in Riyadh and shares educational information to help men understand treatment options, preparation, safety and aftercare."},{"t":"cta","l":"Contact Nurse James","m":"Hello Nurse James, I would like to ask about men's laser hair removal.","v":"pri"}]},{"id":"faq","show":true,"title":"Frequently asked questions","blocks":[{"t":"faq"}]},{"id":"contact","show":true,"title":"Location and contact","blocks":[{"t":"contact","l":"Ask About Availability","m":"Hello, I would like to ask about availability for men's laser hair removal at Harmony Derma in Riyadh."}]}],"areas":[{"n":"Full face","d":"Treats the whole face. Often chosen by men who prefer a clean-shaven look.","show":true},{"n":"Beard shaping and lining","d":"Defines cheek and neck lines. The shape is agreed with you before treatment.","show":true},{"n":"Neck and nape","d":"May help with shaving irritation and razor bumps in this area.","show":true},{"n":"Underarms","d":"A smaller area with shorter sessions.","show":true},{"n":"Chest","d":"A larger area where hair is often thicker.","show":true},{"n":"Abdomen","d":"Often treated together with the chest.","show":true},{"n":"Back","d":"Hard to reach and hard to shave, so often treated professionally.","show":true},{"n":"Shoulders","d":"Frequently planned together with the back.","show":true},{"n":"Full arms","d":"Upper arms and forearms.","show":true},{"n":"Hands and fingers","d":"Fine hair on the backs of the hands and fingers.","show":true},{"n":"Legs and thighs","d":"Large areas, so sessions take longer.","show":true},{"n":"Feet and toes","d":"Small areas on the feet and toes.","show":true},{"n":"Buttocks","d":"Treated privately and professionally.","show":true},{"n":"Intimate areas","d":"Covered in more detail below. The exact area is agreed beforehand.","show":true}],"faqs":[{"q":"What is laser hair removal for men?","a":"A treatment that uses concentrated light to damage hair follicles and reduce hair growth on the face and body.","show":true},{"q":"How does laser hair removal work?","a":"Pigment in the hair absorbs the light and turns it into heat. The heat damages the follicle, so hair grows back finer and slower, or not at all.","show":true},{"q":"Is laser hair removal permanent?","a":"It offers long-term hair reduction, but permanent removal cannot be guaranteed. Some hairs may return, and maintenance sessions may be needed.","show":true},{"q":"How many sessions do men usually need?","a":"Many plans involve around 6 to 10 sessions or more, depending on the person and the area. This is a general estimate, not a guarantee.","show":true},{"q":"How often should men have laser sessions?","a":"Intervals vary by area and are usually several weeks apart, so new hairs can reach the growth phase. Your clinic sets the schedule for you.","show":true},{"q":"Is laser hair removal safe for darker skin?","a":"It can be suitable with careful assessment, appropriate equipment and carefully chosen settings. Darker skin has a higher risk of pigment changes if treated incorrectly, so a consultation matters.","show":true},{"q":"Can men treat their full body?","a":"Many areas can be treated, and treatment can be planned across several areas. Suitability is assessed individually for each person and area.","show":true},{"q":"Can men have laser hair removal on intimate areas?","a":"Often, after an assessment and after agreeing the exact area beforehand. Not every area of intimate skin is automatically suitable.","show":true},{"q":"Does laser hair removal hurt?","a":"Most people describe brief heat, tingling or a rubber-band snap. Sensitivity varies by area and person, and treatment is not painless.","show":true},{"q":"Can laser hair removal cause burns?","a":"Burns and blistering are possible, especially when settings, equipment, skin assessment or aftercare are not appropriate. Careful assessment and following instructions help lower the risk.","show":true},{"q":"Can gray or white hair be treated?","a":"Laser targets pigment, so gray, white and very light hair often responds poorly. Ask for an assessment before planning treatment.","show":true},{"q":"Should I shave before my session?","a":"Usually yes, but follow your clinic's instructions on timing. Avoid waxing or plucking before treatment unless you are told otherwise.","show":true},{"q":"What should I avoid after laser hair removal?","a":"Follow your clinic's advice. Commonly, that means avoiding excess heat, friction and harsh products on treated skin, unprotected sun, and waxing or plucking between sessions.","show":true},{"q":"Can I exercise after a laser session?","a":"Heavy exercise and heat can aggravate treated skin, so many clinics advise a short break. Ask your clinic how long that should be for you.","show":true},{"q":"When will I start noticing results?","a":"Treated hairs often shed over the following weeks, and thinner, slower regrowth tends to build up over the course of sessions. Timing varies.","show":true},{"q":"Why does hair sometimes grow back?","a":"Not every follicle is in the growth phase at each session, hormones can wake dormant follicles, and some hairs respond less to laser.","show":true},{"q":"Are maintenance sessions necessary?","a":"Some men need occasional maintenance sessions to keep their results. It depends on hair, hormones and the area treated.","show":true},{"q":"Where can I inquire about men's laser hair removal in Riyadh?","a":"Message Nurse James on WhatsApp at +966557401562 to ask about treatment at Harmony Derma, Khalid Bin Walid Street, Al Hamra. A message does not confirm an appointment.","show":true}],"pages":[{"id":"pg-privacy","slug":"privacy","title":"Privacy Policy","show":true,"foot":true,"seoT":"","seoD":"","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"This website does not collect personal information on a server. Saved articles are stored only in your own browser, and you can clear them in your browser settings."},{"t":"p","x":"When you open WhatsApp or Instagram from this site, their own terms and privacy policies apply. Please do not share sensitive medical details in public messages."},{"t":"p","x":"If forms or analytics are added later, this policy will be updated first."}]},{"id":"pg-terms","slug":"terms","title":"Terms of Use","show":true,"foot":true,"seoT":"","seoD":"","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"This website provides general educational information about men's laser hair removal. Content may change without notice."},{"t":"p","x":"Sending a WhatsApp message is an inquiry only. It does not confirm an appointment or create a treatment agreement."},{"t":"p","x":"Suitability, safety and results differ between individuals."}]},{"id":"pg-disclaimer","slug":"disclaimer","title":"Medical Disclaimer","show":true,"foot":true,"seoT":"","seoD":"","blocks":[{"t":"p","x":"The information on this website is educational. It does not replace an individual professional assessment, diagnosis or treatment advice."},{"t":"p","x":"Suitability, safety and results depend on your skin, hair, health history and medications. Follow the instructions of your treating clinic."},{"t":"p","x":"Seek prompt medical assessment for severe pain, blistering, worsening swelling or signs of infection. In an emergency, contact your local emergency services."}]}]},"posts":[{"id":"s1","cat":"Basics","date":"2026-10-12","author":"Nurse James","title":"How laser hair removal works for men","body":"Laser hair removal uses a concentrated beam of light that is absorbed by melanin, the pigment in hair. The light turns to heat inside the follicle and damages the structure that grows the hair, so it grows back finer and slower, or stops.\n\n## Why contrast matters\n\nThe laser looks for pigment, so it works best on dark, coarse hair, which is common on men. Different laser types suit different skin tones, which is why a skin and hair assessment comes first.\n\n> The goal is long-term hair reduction, not a single visit.\n\nMost men see hair thin out over a course of sessions. Results vary with hair thickness, hormones, skin tone and the area treated."},{"id":"s2","cat":"Basics","date":"2026-10-09","author":"Nurse James","title":"The hair growth cycle and why you need several sessions","body":"Hair grows in three phases. Anagen is active growth, catagen is a short transition, and telogen is rest. At any moment, only part of the hair in an area is in anagen.\n\n## Why one session is not enough\n\nLaser works best on hairs in the growth phase, when the follicle is connected to the pigmented hair. Hairs resting in telogen are missed, and they enter the growth phase on their own schedule.\n\n- Sessions are spaced weeks apart so new hairs can reach the growth phase\n- Most men need a course of several sessions, and your nurse sets the number after assessing you\n- Gaps are usually shorter on the face and longer on the back and legs\n\nKeeping to the schedule is the biggest factor you control."},{"id":"s3","cat":"Preparation","date":"2026-10-05","author":"Nurse James","title":"Your first session: a preparation checklist for men","body":"A little preparation makes treatment safer and more comfortable. Your nurse will confirm the details for your skin, but these basics apply to most men.\n\n## Before you arrive\n\n- Shave the area the day before, so the laser reaches the root and not the length of the hair\n- Do not wax, pluck or thread for about four weeks beforehand, because the root must be present\n- Avoid sunbathing, tanning beds and self-tanner for two weeks beforehand\n- Skip creams, deodorant and cologne on the treated area\n- Tell your nurse about medications, skin conditions and recent sun exposure\n\n> Arrive with clean, dry, freshly shaved skin.\n\nIf you are unsure about a product or medication, ask before your appointment."},{"id":"s4","cat":"Aftercare","date":"2026-10-01","author":"Nurse James","title":"Aftercare: the first 48 hours","body":"Treated skin can look slightly pink and feel warm, a bit like mild sunburn. This is normal and usually settles within hours to a couple of days. Treated hairs then shed over the following one to three weeks, which is not regrowth.\n\n## What helps\n\n- A cool compress on warm or red areas\n- A gentle, fragrance-free moisturizer\n- Sunscreen every day on exposed areas, which matters a lot in the Riyadh sun\n\n## What to avoid for 48 hours\n\n- Hot showers, saunas and heavy workouts\n- Scrubbing, exfoliating and picking at the skin\n- Waxing and plucking between sessions\n\nContact your clinic if you notice blistering, severe pain or redness that is getting worse."},{"id":"s5","cat":"Areas","date":"2026-09-27","author":"Nurse James","title":"Beard line, neck, chest and back: where men treat most","body":"Men ask for laser on a wide range of areas, and each behaves a little differently.\n\n## Beard line and neck\n\nA clean, sharp edge without daily razor work. Treating the neck can also reduce razor bumps and ingrown hairs. Because results last, your nurse agrees the beard line with you before treatment.\n\n## Chest, back and shoulders\n\nLarger areas with thicker hair. Sessions take longer, and results build up over the course.\n\n## Underarms, arms and legs\n\nCommon for comfort and for athletes who want a smoother, lower-maintenance routine.\n\nMost men start with one or two areas and add more once they know what to expect."},{"id":"s6","cat":"Compare","date":"2026-09-22","author":"Nurse James","title":"Laser vs shaving vs waxing: an honest comparison","body":"Each method has a place. Here is how they compare in everyday use.\n\n## Shaving\n\nCheap and fast, but needed daily or near daily, and it can cause razor bumps, ingrown hairs and irritation.\n\n## Waxing\n\nSmoother for a few weeks, but painful, and ingrown hairs are common.\n\n## Laser\n\nHigher upfront cost and several visits, then far less hair and less maintenance. It reduces hair significantly and for the long term, but no clinic should promise every hair is gone for good, and occasional maintenance sessions may help.\n\n> Ask any clinic what results are realistic for your skin and hair.\n\nIf you shave most days, laser can give you back a lot of time over the years."}]}</script>
